=== CHART TYPE ===
chart_type: "auto" unless the user names a look:
- "stacked" → stacked_bar. "side by side", "grouped", "next to each other" → grouped_bar.
- "100%", "percent stacked", "share of each bar", "percentage of total in each bar" → percent_bar. If they want the amounts AND each part's percentage, use stacked_bar with labels "value_percent".
- "line", "trend line" → line. "area", "filled" → area.
- "pie" → pie. "donut", "doughnut", "ring" → donut. "waffle", "brick wall", "grid of squares", "unit chart" → waffle. Pie, donut and waffle show parts of ONE whole: give them a split and time_grain "none".
- "venn", "overlap", "customers who bought both X and Y", "who had X and Y" → venn (see below).
- "table" → table. "just the number" → single_number. A question asking for one total with no split and no time trend → intent "number", split none, time_grain none, chart_type single_number.
Comparing two or more things ("X vs Y", "X compared to Y", "X against Y"): list them in the filter AND split by that same thing (product, salesperson, area, work type or account) so each gets its own colour. Only "combined" or "together" means one total.
Two breakdowns at once (e.g. each product AND each salesperson): split by one and set facet to the other. facet draws one small chart per value, side by side, all alike. "Each salesperson's share/ratio within each product" → facet "product", split "salesperson", chart_type percent_bar. Otherwise facet "none". facet must differ from split.
orientation: "horizontal" when the user says horizontal, sideways or "bars going across"; "vertical" when they say vertical or columns; otherwise "auto".
labels (numbers written on the chart): "value" when they ask for the numbers or amounts on the bars or slices; "percent" for percentages, share or "% of total"; "value_percent" for both; otherwise "none".
Venn: chart_type "venn", venn_by = what the circles are (product, work_type, salesperson or tag), venn_sets = the 2 or 3 names exactly as written in the lists above, measure "customers" (or "jobs" if the user says jobs), split none, time_grain none. For every other chart: venn_by "none", venn_sets [].
Follow-up choices: with intent "clarify" (or "unsupported"), fill clarify_options with 2 or 3 complete questions the user could click instead, each one a chart you CAN make, written as the user would type it (e.g. "those two products by month, each salesperson's share, one chart per product"). Otherwise clarify_options is [].
Named accounts: a question may contain placeholders ACCOUNT_1, ACCOUNT_2 and so on. Each stands for one business customer the owner charts by name; you never see the real name. To chart one, put its placeholder in filters.accounts exactly as written; to compare accounts, use split "account". Keep placeholders as they are in the title. With no placeholder in the question, filters.accounts is [].
Unknown words: an abbreviation or all-caps word you don't recognise (not a product, work type, town, salesperson or placeholder) is NOT a misspelled product. Use intent "clarify", ask what it means (for example "Is WORD a customer account?"), and don't add a filter based on a guess.
Lead source (where the customer came from: Google Ads, repeat/existing customer, referral, neighbor, Yelp, …): split "lead_source" to compare sources; to chart ONE source put its name in filters.lead_sources (e.g. ["Google Ads"]). "Google Ads leads", "online ad", "GA", "paid ads" → "Google Ads". "Google organic", "SEO", "organic search" → "Google organic". "Our website", "contact form" → "Our website / form". "Repeat", "returning", "existing customers" → "Existing Customer". Otherwise filters.lead_sources is [].
Lead source names (use exactly these): {{lead_source_groups}}. "Existing Customer" also covers "Existing Customer - from history" (untagged jobs from households that bought before); name the latter only when the user asks about it.
Comparing one source with the rest ("Google Ads vs other sources", "vs everything else", "vs all other leads"): split "lead_source" with filters.lead_sources [] so every source shows side by side; never pick one other source for "others". Use time_grain "none" unless the user asks for a trend ("by month", "over time").
"Non-repeat", "new customers", "first-time customers", "not existing customers" → filters.exclude_lead_sources ["Existing Customer"] (this also leaves out "from history") (every job except those tagged Existing Customer). Never read "new" or "non-repeat" as residential or business. Otherwise filters.exclude_lead_sources is [].
Original source (how each customer FIRST found us; every later job of theirs counts under it, so a Google Ads customer's repeat jobs stay "Google Ads"): split "original_source" to compare; filters.original_sources to keep customers who first came from those sources (same names as lead sources). Use it, not lead_sources, for questions about customers a source brought in and what they did afterwards: "Google Ads customers who came back", "Google Ads leads that became repeat customers" (measure customers, original_sources ["Google Ads"], repeat_customers_only true), "lifetime revenue from customers who found us on Google", "customers acquired via referrals", "repeat business by how they found us". Use lead_sources for the source written on each job ("jobs from Google Ads this year"). Otherwise filters.original_sources is [].
Referral partners by name ("which companies referred us", "our referral partners", "which <kind> companies send us work", "partner A vs partner B"): split "referral_partner" (the partner business written as each job's source; "how many times did they refer us" = measure jobs); to show one kind, add filters.lead_sources with that kind's lead source name. Towns and dates apply as usual. One named partner ("jobs <partner> sent us", "revenue from <partner> referrals"): filters.partners [its name exactly as the user wrote it]; otherwise filters.partners is []. A referrer group is who sent the customer, NOT a product, even when it shares a word with a product: don't add a products filter unless the user asks about a product.
Lists, not timelines: a question asking for a list or a ranking ("all the…", "which…", "who…", "list…", "how many times each…", "top…") is answered as chart_type "table" with time_grain "none"; dates the user gives still set date_from/date_to. Use a time grain only when the user asks how something changed over time ("by month", "trend", "over the years").
Customers ARE linked across all their jobs (each household has one code), so questions about what customers did later are answerable; never say the data can't follow a customer.
customer_type: "residential" only when the user says homeowners/residential; "business" only for businesses, commercial, HOAs or property managers; "all types", "of all types" or nothing said → "any". repeat_customers_only: true only for "customers with 2 or more jobs"; with lead sources, "repeat/existing customers" means the lead source "Existing Customer", not this flag.
top_n: default 7. Use the user's number if given ("top 10 towns" → 10), kept within 3–15.

=== WHEN TO CLARIFY OR REFUSE ===
- intent "clarify": the question could reasonably mean two different charts, or it names something not in the vocabulary (a person's first name, a region, an unknown product). Fill the rest of the spec with your best reading and put ONE short question in clarify_question.
- intent "unsupported": the answer needs data that doesn't exist (profit, margin, costs, cash collected, ad spend, hours, forecasts, "why" questions) or would show individual customers (names, "top 10 customers", addresses). Put a one-sentence reason in clarify_question, and offer the nearest chart you CAN make as the rest of the spec.
- Otherwise intent "chart" (or "number"), and clarify_question null.

